[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Data-Driven and Keyword-Driven Framework Architectures in Selenium"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"As test automation suites scale, maintaining hardcoded test scripts becomes unsustainable. Advanced test automation architectures like Data-Driven and Keyword-Driven frameworks help solve this challenge by decoupling test data and test logic from the automation script execution engine."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Data-Driven Framework Architecture"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In a Data-Driven Framework, test data is separated from the test scripts and stored in external repositories such as Excel spreadsheets, CSV files, JSON/YAML files, or databases. The same test script is executed multiple times with different sets of test data."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reusability: A single test script can validate multiple input scenarios (e.g., valid and invalid logins)."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Maintainability: Changes in test data do not require modifying the source code."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Scalability: Adding new test data combinations simply involves adding rows to the data source."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing Data-Driven Testing using Apache POI in Java"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Below is an example of a utility class designed to read test data from an Excel sheet using Apache POI, which can then be integrated with TestNG's @DataProvider."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"public class ExcelUtils {
    public static Object[][] getTestData(String excelPath, String sheetName) throws IOException {
        FileInputStream fis = new FileInputStream(excelPath);
        Workbook workbook = new XSSFWorkbook(fis);
        Sheet sheet = workbook.getSheet(sheetName);
        int rowCount = sheet.getPhysicalNumberOfRows();
        int colCount = sheet.getRow(0).getLastCellNum();
        Object[][] data = new Object[rowCount - 1][colCount];
        for (int i = 1; i < rowCount; i++) {
            Row row = sheet.getRow(i);
            for (int j = 0; j < colCount; j++) {
                Cell cell = row.getCell(j);
                data[i - 1][j] = cell.toString();
            }
        }
        workbook.close();
        fis.close();
        return data;
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Keyword-Driven Framework Architecture"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"A Keyword-Driven Framework is an application-independent framework where the execution of test steps is driven by keywords (e.g., OPEN_BROWSER, CLICK, ENTER_TEXT, VERIFY_TITLE). These keywords are defined in an external table or file, allowing non-technical manual testers or business analysts to write and understand automated test scenarios."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Abstraction: Hides the underlying Selenium implementation complexity behind descriptive keywords."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Collaboration: Bridges the gap between technical automation engineers and domain experts."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Flexibility: Modifications to UI elements only require updates to the keyword engine, not the test sheets."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing a Keyword Engine in Selenium"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"The core of a keyword-driven framework uses a Java reflection or switch-case mechanism to parse instructions from an Excel sheet or property file and invoke the corresponding Selenium actions."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"public class ActionKeywords {
    public WebDriver driver;

    public void openBrowser(String browserName) {
        if (browserName.equalsIgnoreCase("chrome")) {
            driver = new ChromeDriver();
        } else if (browserName.equalsIgnoreCase("firefox")) {
            driver = new FirefoxDriver();
        }
    }

    public void navigate(String url) {
        driver.get(url);
    }

    public void inputData(String locator, String value) {
        driver.findElement(By.xpath(locator)).sendKeys(value);
    }

    public void clickElement(String locator) {
        driver.findElement(By.xpath(locator)).click();
    }
}"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Hybrid Framework Architecture"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"In real-world enterprise automation, teams rarely use a single design pattern. Instead, they implement a Hybrid Framework that combines the best of both worlds: Data-Driven testing for feeding variations of data into tests, and Keyword-Driven structuring for defining reusable business workflows."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Combines modularity from keyword-driven setups with parameterization from data-driven models."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Integrates robust reporting mechanisms (Extent Reports, Allure) and parallel execution support via TestNG or JUnit."}]
